  "prediction": "**Mountain Walker Dies in Snowdonia Accident**\n\nA 53-year-old woman, Jane Wilson, fell to her death while attempting to find a safe route down from the Tryfan peak in Snowdonia, North Wales. The incident occurred on March 25, when Wilson and her husband, Gary, decided not to reach the summit due to approaching dusk.\n\nUsing only Gary's smartphone to navigate, they headed across the mountain's west face, known for its loose rocks and steep inclines. Jane Wilson, an experienced mountain walker and academic engagement librarian at the University of Manchester, went ahead to look for a suitable route. After asking her husband if it was safe, she fell 20-30 feet down a vertical cliff and tumbled about 150 meters down into an area known as Notch arrete.\n\nGary Wilson, who was following behind, realized his wife had fallen and climbed to a safer ledge to raise the alarm. Members of the Ogwen Valley Mountain Rescue Team were called to the scene and eventually led Gary to safety. Unfortunately, Jane Wilson's body was found, and she had sustained severe injuries, including a fractured skull.\n\nThe inquest into Jane Wilson's death heard that the couple had been mountain walking and scrambling for six years and that Jane was a fit and active woman. However, the coroner recorded a conclusion of accidental death, citing the use of a smartphone app to navigate the mountain as a contributing factor.\n\nExperts have warned that relying solely on electronic navigation aids can be hazardous, especially in areas with limited detail or poor signal. Mountain Rescue England and Wales has urged walkers to use a map and compass in addition to electronic navigation aids, citing the potential difficulties caused by poor detail on electronic maps and the risk of batteries running out at critical moments.\n\nThe incident has highlighted the importance of proper planning and preparation when engaging in mountain walking and scrambling. Tryfan, in particular, is known to be a challenging peak, with about 30% of incidents attended by the Ogwen Rescue team occurring on this mountain.",
